Reading, for popularity -- the cue whose null this section is about:
  * first  app +0.45, effect present
  * second app -0.22, inconclusive
  * third  app +0.40, inconclusive
  * third vs first: difference 0.05, p = 0.86 (Holm)
  * smallest Holm-corrected p between positions, any cue: 0.066
So 'popularity works before comparison and dissolves after' is not what the
data says. The drop is confined to the second app, no pairwise difference
survives Holm, and the +0.07 'at the boundary' verdict above is an average
of two inconclusive estimates of opposite sign. What survives is narrower:
popularity moves intention on first exposure, and after that this design
cannot say -- which is still enough to deny the 2023 claim that popularity
is ineffective throughout.


